n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(({3}^{2})^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314972 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315058 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 31\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=31,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 31\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=31,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315059 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 18 & -21 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-18{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+6(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-3){a}_{2}+(-1){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1)=6.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 18 & -21 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-18{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+6(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-3){a}_{2}+(-1){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1)=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317208 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ 7s+10t &=9 \\\\ -3s-4t &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ t &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b-\\frac{1}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1.$" "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ 7s+10t &=9 \\\\ -3s-4t &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ t &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b-\\frac{1}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317213 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317320 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” are false, while statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” is true." "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” are false, while statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186317403 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{4} &=11 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{4} &=11 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317416 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=4 \\\\ 0 &=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=4 \\\\ 0 &=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317417 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[F\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2} &=-14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=1.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot (-2)=-2.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[F\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2} &=-14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=1.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot (-2)=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319150 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is false, while statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” are true." "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is false, while statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186319157 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the 